\title{cpaic: Component-Based Population-Adjusted Indirect Comparison}
\description{
Experimental research software that combines component network meta-analysis (cNMA) with population-adjusted indirect comparison (PAIC) methods for disconnected treatment networks. The additive component structure of cNMA (Ruecker et al. 2020 \doi{10.1002/bimj.201800167}) can bridge sub-networks that share treatment components. Anchored simulated treatment comparison (STC) and matching-adjusted indirect comparison (MAIC) adjust individual-patient-data contrasts before a two-stage component bridge. This can mix target-specific contrasts with retained aggregate contrasts from different study populations; marginal contrasts on nonlinear scales also need not be component-additive, so affected bridges require explicit experimental opt-in. Component-additive multilevel network meta-regression (ML-NMR; Phillippo et al. 2020 \doi{10.1111/rssa.12579}) jointly models individual and aggregate data. Post-fit reporting includes average conditional effects on the link scale at supplied covariate means and treatment-level marginal contrasts standardized over an explicit target covariate distribution, with empirical or named summary targets, supported continuous margins, and moment and joint-dependence fidelity checks. Survival summaries begin at model time zero and use donor-study follow-up support; nonlinear marginal effects remain treatment-level summaries. Models are implemented for binary, continuous, count, and time-to-event outcomes. This methodology and implementation have not been validated for clinical, regulatory, reimbursement, or other decision use.
}
